#Introduction
Platforms in the gaming industry have evolved drastically over many decades where things like conenience and accessibility to games have improved and changed the way people interact with and use gaming technology. However the evolution of systems and jumps to new technology to access
gaming have created ethical challenges with consumer rights, accessibility and privacy. Here we are going to look at multiple different areas where there is concern around ethical issues with the evolution of gaming.

#Age Restricted Content
In the days of arcade gaming, anyone could walk in and play whatever video game the place had to offer, as graphics got better there came more violent games such as mortal kombat, this created concern over kids being able to play these and view sometimes extremely graphic content without any kind of restriction. With home gaming copies of violent games there was concern over the same issue, which is what led to PEGI becoming a thing in europe where age ratings have to be displayed on a game so stores can check a customers age before selling. Digital purchases face this issue too with kids being able to fake their age and gain access to parent accounts to buy age restricted content, to prevent this stronger parental controls and purchase approvals using things like digital id verification can help reduce problems like this.


#Physical and Digital game ownership / access to patches and fixes
Digital game ownership has been a revolutionary advancement in the industry which allowed users to access games without a physcial disc or cartridge copy through an online serbice. For example, on playstation this service and the players access to their product is dependant on Sonys online service to verify their licence whenever they want to use it, this creates a lot of concern to people around owning the product they bought and the level of access they truly have to it. There is also concern around if a game is released with issues or bugs that need fixing it can only be fixed through online updates that might not be available to someone who purchased a physical copy of the game and has no access to wifi. As of recent years on xbox and playstation discs only work as keys for the user to download and play the game which links into the no wifi issue, while nintendo have stuck to the traditional way of the games files being on the cartridges storage there is still concern about the need for patches and updates. The european commission has noticed that if a game company decides to shut their servers down it can cause parts of or the entire game to become unplayable, this usually happens with older modern titles that rely on online play and a company usually decides to do this for a number of reasons but usally is them losing money from keeping it active. Membership services such as xbox game pass allow users to access a massive library of games to download and play as long as they are actively subscribed to the service, the catch is that microsoft could randomly remove a game from the membership at any point, leaving players without access and forced to buy the title if they want to continue playing, which can cause frustration on top of already costly membership fees especially if they invested lots of time into the game, Microsoft have helped this concern by giving notice period for the removal of games. Cloud gaming

