#Hardware Failures
When people used to mainly go to the arcade to play video games, machines would occasionally go out of order for hardware or software related reasons. If this happened it was not too much of a concern to the players as the arcade would be responsible for servicing and repairing the machines, with the move to home gaming hardware failures became more of a concern. A lot of consoles made in the 80s and 90s such as the NES, Sega and playstation systems were engineered well and functionally held up well past warranty, but when the xbox 360 first released it had a very common fatal hardware failure on most consoles called the red ring of death. This left a mark on microsofts reputation as it left most people who had spent hundreds on the new console with a useless and completely disfunctional piece of technology. Microsoft fought this issue by replacing the broken consoles and the issue would keep on occurring even with new models. This greatly affected everyone who owned a 360 in its early days and went on to make companies work more to prevent this from happening again. 

